        "description": "Beginning with an introduction summarizing critical reaction to Pynchon's fiction and to Gravity's Rainbow in particular, this volume contains eight original essays exploring different aspects of the novel. Alan Friedman provides a guide to the novel's use of statistics and post-Newtonian physics and Khachig Toloyan examines Pynchon's use of history. Charles Cler explores the author's use of film; Joseph Slade discusses his treatment of religion, psychology, sex and love; Raymond Olderman discusses Pynchon's contrast of freak and straight world views; James Earl writes on Pynchon's opposition of rational and intuitive epistemologies in the novel; Charles Russell on Pynchon's portrayal of the limitations of the language systems and Roger Henkle on comedy. ISBN 0-8242-0337-X : $25.00.",
